},
{
  files: ["**/*.{ts,tsx,mts,mjs}"],
  ignores: ["lib/backend-client.ts"],
  rules: {
    "no-restricted-imports": [
      "error",
      { paths: [{ name: "openapi-fetch", message: BACKEND_ACCESS }] },
    ],
  },
},
```

And `frontend/tests/lint-rules.test.ts` asserts these rules **actually fire** on planted code.

### 6.4 How to fetch `GET /api/personal/digest`

The path **already exists in the generated schema** — `frontend/lib/api/schema.ts:314`:

```ts
"/api/personal/digest": {
    parameters: { query?: never; header?: never; path?: never; cookie?: never; };
    /**
     * The signed-in user's Pulse Digest for today
     * @description The user's Pulse Digest for their local day, as it was composed.
     *
     *     The response always says which state it is in: `composed` (there is a digest and it has cards), `empty` (there is a digest and it has none — nothing qualifying near you today), or `not_composed` (the digest has not been built yet). The last two are different facts and the client shows different things for them.
     *
     *     Each card carries the event's name, time and place, up to three Why-this Tag keys, the Confidence Pulse bucket (1-5) and the Source Badge variant. The bucket is sent rather than the raw score: the bucket the user saw this morning is a persisted fact about their day, and a client that re-bucketed it could flicker.
     *
     *     The times are instants plus an IANA timezone; converting them for display is the client's job. `catalog_event_missing` marks a card whose event the catalog no longer holds — the explanation is still there, because a composition is a fact about the day.
     */
    get: operations["apps_personalization_api_read_digest_endpoint"];
```

The operation returns `components["schemas"]["DigestOut"]` with **200 only**.

**`DigestOut`** (`schema.ts:995`):

```ts
DigestOut: {
    cards: components["schemas"]["DigestCardOut"][];
    city?: string | null;
    city_source?: string | null;
    /** Format: date */
    local_date: string;
    /** @enum {string} */
    status: "composed" | "empty" | "not_composed";
    timezone: string;
};
```

**`DigestCardOut`** (`schema.ts:926`):

```ts
DigestCardOut: {
    bucket: number;
    canonical_url?: string | null;
    catalog_event_missing: boolean;
    conflict: boolean;
    end_time_utc?: string | null;
    event_id: string;
    event_kind: string;
    location?: string | null;
    slot: string;
    source_badge?: string | null;
    start_time_tz?: string | null;
    start_time_utc?: string | null;
    tags: string[];
    title?: string | null;
    tz_review_pending: boolean;
    venue_city?: string | null;
    venue_name?: string | null;
};
```

The schema's own contract comments are decisive for the Pulse Card:

> **The bucket, not the score.** FR-22's raw `relevance_score` is an internal number; the card shows 1-5 dots. Sending the score would invite the frontend to re-bucket it … which would break the no-flicker rule, since the bucket the user saw this morning is a **persisted fact about their day**.
>
> **The tags are the persisted keys, in the persisted order.** They are not recomputed and not re-sorted — the order is the priority order … The client owns the labels.
>
> **The times are instants plus the IANA zone.** AD-6: "display-time conversion happens at the presentation edge, never in domain logic".
>
> **`source_badge` is the variant key, or `None`.** A personal event has no source, so it has no badge; `None` here means "there is no source", not "unknown". The client owns the label and the glyph.

**The call:**

```ts
// Server component (the pattern from saved-events-section.tsx)
const response = await api.GET("/api/personal/digest", {
  headers: await sessionHeaders(),
});
const digest = response.data;   // DigestOut
```

Note `sessionHeaders()` is **required** — without it the Next.js server's own (empty) cookie jar makes the read anonymous.

